PG.notes("l348", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains the difference between a coin (own blockchain) and a token (built on an existing chain), why tokens are far cheaper to launch, and what tokens are used for. Sets up the section project: a custom token called DANG (a play on the instructor's name) on the Internet Computer.</p>\n<h3>Coin vs token</h3>\n<ul><li>A coin runs on its own blockchain. Creating one means standing up a chain (often by forking an existing one) and then convincing enough miners/validators to secure it.</li><li>A token is deployed onto a chain that already exists (Ethereum, the Internet Computer). No new chain, no miners to recruit; the token is just code on the host chain.</li><li>Examples given: Dogecoin is a coin (its own chain, a fork lineage that goes back to Bitcoin via Litecoin-style forks). Shiba Inu is a token because it lives on Ethereum.</li><li>In everyday speech the word token is used for both, including for ICP, even though ICP technically has its own chain.</li></ul>\n<h3>Cost and effort of each route</h3>\n<ul><li>Coin: copying the chain code is easy, but securing it needs many miners, which consumes energy and requires people who believe the coin has a future.</li><li>Token: the only work is writing the ledger (who owns how many units) and the rules for transferring units and recording the change.</li><li>A token can plug into the host chain's existing user base and exchanges (the Shiba example used Ethereum DEXs such as Uniswap), so trading can start immediately instead of waiting to build an exchange ecosystem.</li><li>Any token holder can be sent tokens as long as their public wallet address is known; this is how token creators can seed large amounts to well-known wallets.</li></ul>\n<h3>What tokens are used for</h3>\n<ul><li>Payment: swap one token for another, or pay for a product or service.</li><li>Equity/governance: holders vote on the direction of a decentralised app. On the Internet Computer, ICP can be staked to vote on proposals and earns rewards in return, loosely like shares in a company.</li><li>Utility: a token gives access to a function inside an app or chain. The cycles obtained from the cycles faucet earlier in the course are a utility token that pays for canister computation.</li><li>Representing real-world assets: for example an energy token that small providers trade directly.</li><li>Gaming rewards: in-game tokens earned by playing that can be spent, staked or used to vote on governance.</li></ul>\n<h3>Burning tokens</h3>\n<ul><li>Burning means destroying tokens or sending them to an address nobody can access, which removes them from circulation.</li></ul>\n<h3>What the section builds</h3>\n<ul><li>A custom token named DANG (short for 'Decentralized Angela'), written in Motoko on the Internet Computer, with a ledger, a faucet and transfers.</li><li>Learners are free to pick their own token name and symbol.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling every crypto asset a coin: the distinction depends on whether the asset has its own blockchain.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Treating the market-cap anecdotes as investment guidance; they are context only.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l349", "<p class=\"summary\">Download the pre-built React frontend plus an empty Motoko backend, install dependencies, deploy the empty canister to the local replica and run the dev server so the DSurv token UI (Faucet, Check Balance, Transfer) appears on localhost:8080.</p>\n<h3>What the skeleton contains</h3>\n<ul><li>A React frontend with the HTML/CSS and four components already written: Header, Faucet, Balance and Transfer, composed in App.jsx. Only blockchain logic and Motoko are left for the learner.</li><li>A blank Motoko backend: main.mo has had all starter code removed.</li><li>Pre-configured React tooling: a tsconfig file, adjusted bundler config and extra dependencies in package.json (Dfinity agent/auth packages, React, Material UI icons).</li><li>The visual design is modelled on the retro, old-Macintosh look of Curve Finance (an automated market maker used to swap stablecoins); the demo site is branded DSurv.</li></ul>\n<h3>Run sequence</h3>\n<ul><li>Terminal 1: dfx start launches the local simulated Internet Computer replica.</li><li>Terminal 2: npm install fetches everything listed under dependencies and devDependencies in package.json into node_modules (over a thousand packages). Deprecation warnings (for example the Material UI icons package renamed to MUI) are harmless.</li><li>Terminal 3: dfx deploy creates the wallet canister, builds the backend canister and installs it on the local replica. It also generates the dist and declarations folders, which the frontend needs to talk to the backend. It must run before npm start even though main.mo is empty.</li><li>Then npm start serves the frontend; open localhost:8080 (the URL is printed in the terminal).</li><li>Expected page: header, a Faucet section (claim free tokens), a Check Balance section, and a Transfer section. The browser console should show no errors.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where the next lessons go</h3>\n<ul><li>Next lesson builds the backend ledger and balance check in main.mo, then connects it to the Balance component.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Setup commands, each in its own terminal</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx start        # local replica (leave running)\nnpm install      # install frontend deps\ndfx deploy       # build + install canisters, generates declarations\nnpm start        # serve the frontend, open localhost:8080</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx composition in the skeleton (from reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div id=&quot;screen&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;\n      &lt;Faucet /&gt;\n      &lt;Balance /&gt;\n      &lt;Transfer /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Get the token skeleton running locally</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> token.zip from the lesson resources.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> See the DSurv token frontend on localhost:8080 with no console errors.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download token.zip, unzip it and open the folder in VS Code.</li><li>Run dfx start in one terminal.</li><li>In a second terminal run npm install.</li><li>In a third terminal run dfx deploy and wait for the deployed canisters message.</li><li>Run npm start and open localhost:8080.</li><li>Check that Faucet, Check Balance and Transfer sections render and the console is clean.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A retro-styled page with Faucet, Balance and Transfer panels (non-functional yet).</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Skipping dfx deploy: the declarations folder will not exist and the frontend cannot import the backend.</li><li>Stopping dfx start while working: deploy and the page then fail to reach the replica.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo is a newer dfx scaffold: dfx.json uses a Vite workspace for DANG_frontend (with vite.config.js) rather than the webpack setup in the video, and the project is named DANG. Commands and the port may differ slightly (Vite typically uses 3000/5173 or the port printed by npm start).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l350", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds the token's backend ledger in Motoko: an owner Principal, total supply and symbol, a HashMap from Principal to Nat holding balances, and a balanceOf query that safely unwraps the optional returned by get. Finishes by deploying and calling balanceOf from the command line.</p>\n<h3>Principal: identifying users and canisters</h3>\n<ul><li>Principal is the Internet Computer type that uniquely identifies either a user (identity) or a canister.</li><li>Get your own id as text with dfx identity get-principal; it is a long dash-separated string for the default identity.</li><li>Import the type (import Principal from mo:base/Principal). Principal.fromText converts that string into a real Principal value; a plain Text cannot be used where a Principal is required.</li><li>The owner is the identity that receives the entire initial supply when the canister is created.</li></ul>\n<h3>Actor state: owner, totalSupply, symbol</h3>\n<ul><li>Create an actor named Token in main.mo and define its variables inside it.</li><li>owner : Principal, assigned via Principal.fromText with your own id pasted in (whole string, all dashes).</li><li>totalSupply : Nat, set to one billion (nine zeros). Nat is a natural number, so it can never be negative.</li><li>symbol : Text, the short ticker (DANG here). Tickers imitate currency codes such as USD, GBP or ETH; the learner may choose any name.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer reference code writes these as persistent actor with transient var, which is the Motoko enhanced-orthogonal-persistence syntax; the lecture uses a plain actor and var/let, and either way the ledger in this lesson is not meant to survive upgrades (that is handled in a later lesson).</li></ul>\n<h3>The ledger as a HashMap</h3>\n<ul><li>A ledger here is a store mapping an account id to the amount of tokens it holds. A dictionary-like structure fits, and Motoko's version is HashMap (a hash table): a key is hashed to find where its value lives, so lookups are efficient.</li><li>Import it with import HashMap from mo:base/HashMap (watch the capitals).</li><li>Declare: var balances = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash). The generic arguments are key type then value type.</li><li>Constructor arguments: (1) initial capacity, here 1; (2) an equality function so the map can compare keys, Principal.equal; (3) a hash function for the key type, Principal.hash.</li><li>balances.put(key, value) inserts a value at a key and overwrites any previous entry for that key. Calling balances.put(owner, totalSupply) at the top level of the actor gives the owner the whole supply when the canister is installed.</li></ul>\n<h3>balanceOf query function</h3>\n<ul><li>public query func balanceOf(who : Principal) : async Nat. query means it reads state only and returns fast without going through consensus; async because all public canister functions return a future.</li><li>balances.get(who) returns an Option: ?Nat. If the key is absent the result is null, not a number, so it cannot be returned directly as Nat.</li><li>An Option is a type-safe null: it keeps the underlying type but allows 'no value'. It is the same idea as Swift optionals.</li><li>Unwrap with switch: switch (balances.get(who)) { case null 0; case (?result) result }. The null branch yields 0 (account has no balance); the ?result branch binds the inner Nat to result and drops the question mark.</li><li>Assign the switch result to let balance : Nat and return it. The earlier if-null-then-0 approach becomes unnecessary.</li></ul>\n<h3>Deploying and calling from the CLI</h3>\n<ul><li>Save main.mo then run dfx deploy again; the canister is reinstalled with the owner holding the full supply.</li><li>Candid arguments on the command line must be typed: a Principal argument has to look like principal &quot;id&quot;, not just the id text.</li><li>Build that string once in a shell variable using command substitution, check it with echo, then call the method with dfx canister call token balanceOf and the variable.</li><li>The response shows the billion as a nat, confirming the ledger and balanceOf work. The starter project's README holds copy-paste versions of these commands to avoid typos, plus a place to save your own principal for later lessons.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">main.mo after this lesson (reconstructed; reference repo uses persistent actor + transient var)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\nimport HashMap &quot;mo:base/HashMap&quot;;\n\nactor Token {\n  // Replace with the output of: dfx identity get-principal\n  var owner : Principal = Principal.fromText(&quot;&lt;your-principal-id&gt;&quot;);\n  var totalSupply : Nat = 1000000000;\n  var symbol : Text = &quot;DANG&quot;;\n\n  // ledger: Principal -&gt; balance\n  var balances = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n\n  // runs once at deploy: give the whole supply to the owner\n  balances.put(owner, totalSupply);\n\n  public query func balanceOf(who : Principal) : async Nat {\n    let balance : Nat = switch (balances.get(who)) {\n      case null 0;\n      case (?result) result;\n    };\n    return balance;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">CLI sequence to deploy and check the owner balance</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx identity get-principal            # prints your principal id (text)\ndfx deploy                            # redeploy after editing main.mo\nOWNER_PUBLIC_KEY=&quot;principal \\&quot;$(dfx identity get-principal)\\&quot;&quot;\necho $OWNER_PUBLIC_KEY                # principal &quot;xxxxx-...-xxx&quot;\ndfx canister call token balanceOf &quot;$OWNER_PUBLIC_KEY&quot;   # (1_000_000_000 : nat)</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create the ledger and check the owner's balance</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The skeleton project from the previous lesson with an empty main.mo.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Mint a total supply to your own principal in a HashMap ledger and read it back through a balanceOf query.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run dfx identity get-principal and copy the id.</li><li>In main.mo import Principal and HashMap, then define actor Token.</li><li>Add owner (Principal.fromText with your id), totalSupply (Nat, 1 billion) and symbol (Text).</li><li>Create balances as a HashMap&lt;Principal, Nat&gt; with initial size 1, Principal.equal and Principal.hash.</li><li>Put the owner into balances with the full total supply.</li><li>Write public query func balanceOf(who : Principal) : async Nat that switches on balances.get(who): null gives 0, ?result gives result.</li><li>Run dfx deploy.</li><li>In the terminal create OWNER_PUBLIC_KEY in the principal &quot;id&quot; format, echo it, then dfx canister call token balanceOf with it.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The canister call returns 1_000_000_000 : nat for the owner principal.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Principal &quot;mo:base/Principal&quot;;\nimport HashMap &quot;mo:base/HashMap&quot;;\n\nactor Token {\n  // Replace with the output of: dfx identity get-principal\n  var owner : Principal = Principal.fromText(&quot;&lt;your-principal-id&gt;&quot;);\n  var totalSupply : Nat = 1000000000;\n  var symbol : Text = &quot;DANG&quot;;\n\n  // ledger: Principal -&gt; balance\n  var balances = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n\n  // runs once at deploy: give the whole supply to the owner\n  balances.put(owner, totalSupply);\n\n  public query func balanceOf(who : Principal) : async Nat {\n    let balance : Nat = switch (balances.get(who)) {\n      case null 0;\n      case (?result) result;\n    };\n    return balance;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Passing the principal as plain text to balanceOf: it must be wrapped as principal &quot;...&quot;.</li><li>Forgetting to import HashMap or Principal, or mistyping the capitals.</li><li>Trying to return balances.get(who) directly: it is ?Nat, not Nat.</li><li>Copying only part of the principal string into fromText.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference README sets OWNER_PUBLIC_KEY with single quotes, which stops $(...) from expanding in a shell; use double quotes with escaped inner quotes as in the CLI snippet. Also the correct command spelling is dfx identity get-principal (the README text shows a stray 'get -principal').</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's hard-coded principal is the student's own; use yours.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l351", "<p class=\"summary\">Wires the Balance React component to the Motoko canister: a controlled input holds a principal string, it is converted with the @dfinity/principal library, balanceOf is awaited through the generated declarations, and the formatted result is shown. A challenge then adds a getSymbol query so the ticker appears, and a hidden flag hides the result line until a balance has loaded.</p>\n<h3>Controlled input with useState</h3>\n<ul><li>Balance.jsx already has the markup and a handleClick handler on the Check Balance button; initially the handler only logs to the console.</li><li>To read what the user typed, keep it in state: const [inputValue, setInput] = useState(&quot;&quot;) with an empty string as the starting value.</li><li>Make the input controlled: value={inputValue} plus onChange={(e) =&gt; setInput(e.target.value)}. e is the change event, e.target is the input element that fired it and .value is its current text. This value/onChange pairing is the standard React pattern for form fields.</li><li>Quick check: log inputValue in handleClick and confirm the typed text appears when the button is clicked.</li></ul>\n<h3>Calling the canister from the browser</h3>\n<ul><li>Text from an input is a string, but balanceOf expects a Principal. Convert it first with Principal.fromText(inputValue) from the JavaScript package @dfinity/principal. This mirrors Motoko's Principal module; the @ prefix marks a scoped npm package (a way of grouping related packages) already listed in package.json.</li><li>Import the backend actor from the generated declarations folder: a relative path climbing out of components and src to reach declarations, then the canister's folder (the lecture names it token; the reference repo names it DANG_backend). These declarations are produced by dfx deploy.</li><li>Call it with await: const balance = await token.balanceOf(principal). The function must be async because the call is a network request to the canister.</li><li>The balance arrives as a number-like Nat (a BigInt). To show it in the page convert with balance.toLocaleString(), which returns a string with thousands separators (note the spelling locale, not local).</li><li>Store the display string in state, balanceResult/setBalance, initial value an empty string, and render {balanceResult} inside the result paragraph.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge: show the token symbol</h3>\n<ul><li>Requirement: modify both Motoko and React so the symbol appears after the balance, e.g. '... 1,000,000,000 DANG'.</li><li>Backend: add a public query func getSymbol() : async Text that simply returns the symbol variable. Remember the trailing semicolon after the function body.</li><li>Frontend: add const [cryptoSymbol, setSymbol] = useState(&quot;&quot;); in handleClick call setSymbol(await token.getSymbol()); render {cryptoSymbol} right after {balanceResult}.</li></ul>\n<h3>Redeploy whenever the backend changes</h3>\n<ul><li>Error 'token.getSymbol is not a function' appears if only the React file was saved. npm start hot-reloads the frontend but does not rebuild the canister or regenerate declarations.</li><li>After any change to main.mo, run dfx deploy again so the new method exists on the canister and in the declarations the frontend imports. This will come up repeatedly in ICP development.</li></ul>\n<h3>UX polish: hiding the empty message</h3>\n<ul><li>On first load the sentence 'This account has a balance of .' makes no sense.</li><li>Add const [isHidden, setHidden] = useState(true) and put hidden={isHidden} on the paragraph. The HTML hidden attribute removes the element from view while true.</li><li>At the end of handleClick call setHidden(false) so the line appears only after a balance and symbol were fetched.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Balance.jsx after the lesson (excerpt-level reconstruction; canister import name differs by project)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { Principal } from &quot;@dfinity/principal&quot;;\nimport { token } from &quot;../../../declarations/token&quot;; // reference repo: DANG_backend\n\nfunction Balance() {\n  const [inputValue, setInput] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [balanceResult, setBalance] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [cryptoSymbol, setSymbol] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [isHidden, setHidden] = useState(true);\n\n  async function handleClick() {\n    const principal = Principal.fromText(inputValue);\n    const balance = await token.balanceOf(principal);\n    setBalance(balance.toLocaleString());\n    setSymbol(await token.getSymbol());\n    setHidden(false);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;window white&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;label&gt;Check account token balance:&lt;/label&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;\n        &lt;input\n          id=&quot;balance-principal-id&quot;\n          type=&quot;text&quot;\n          placeholder=&quot;Enter a Principal ID&quot;\n          value={inputValue}\n          onChange={(e) =&gt; setInput(e.target.value)}\n        /&gt;\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;p className=&quot;trade-buttons&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;button id=&quot;btn-request-balance&quot; onClick={handleClick}&gt;\n          Check Balance\n        &lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;p hidden={isHidden}&gt;\n        This account has a balance of {balanceResult} {cryptoSymbol}.\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Balance;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">New Motoko query added in the challenge</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">  public query func getSymbol() : async Text {\n    return symbol;\n  };</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Redeploy after backend edits</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx deploy</pre>\n<h2>The task: Show the token symbol next to the balance</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Balance.jsx wired to balanceOf, showing only the number; main.mo with balanceOf.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> After clicking Check Balance the page shows the formatted balance followed by the token symbol, and the sentence is hidden until a check is done.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In main.mo add public query func getSymbol() : async Text returning symbol.</li><li>Run dfx deploy to publish the new method and regenerate declarations.</li><li>In Balance.jsx add cryptoSymbol/setSymbol state, empty-string initial value.</li><li>In handleClick call setSymbol(await token.getSymbol()).</li><li>Render {cryptoSymbol} after {balanceResult} in the result paragraph.</li><li>Add isHidden/setHidden state (true), set hidden={isHidden} on the paragraph and call setHidden(false) at the end of handleClick.</li><li>Paste a principal id, click Check Balance, and confirm the line appears with the symbol.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> 'This account has a balance of 1,000,000,000 DANG.' appears only after clicking; before that no text shows under the button.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { Principal } from &quot;@dfinity/principal&quot;;\nimport { token } from &quot;../../../declarations/token&quot;; // reference repo: DANG_backend\n\nfunction Balance() {\n  const [inputValue, setInput] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [balanceResult, setBalance] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [cryptoSymbol, setSymbol] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [isHidden, setHidden] = useState(true);\n\n  async function handleClick() {\n    const principal = Principal.fromText(inputValue);\n    const balance = await token.balanceOf(principal);\n    setBalance(balance.toLocaleString());\n    setSymbol(await token.getSymbol());\n    setHidden(false);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;window white&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;label&gt;Check account token balance:&lt;/label&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;\n        &lt;input\n          id=&quot;balance-principal-id&quot;\n          type=&quot;text&quot;\n          placeholder=&quot;Enter a Principal ID&quot;\n          value={inputValue}\n          onChange={(e) =&gt; setInput(e.target.value)}\n        /&gt;\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;p className=&quot;trade-buttons&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;button id=&quot;btn-request-balance&quot; onClick={handleClick}&gt;\n          Check Balance\n        &lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;p hidden={isHidden}&gt;\n        This account has a balance of {balanceResult} {cryptoSymbol}.\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Balance;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Passing the raw string to balanceOf instead of a Principal object: convert with Principal.fromText.</li><li>Forgetting dfx deploy after editing main.mo, producing 'is not a function' errors.</li><li>Misspelling toLocaleString as toLocalString.</li><li>Calling Principal.fromText with an empty or invalid string throws, so clicking with a blank field fails. (Extra) Wrap it in try/catch or validate first.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo imports DANG_backend from declarations, not token; use your own canister name. Its main.mo also marks balances as transient, a newer persistence syntax not used in the video.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l352", "<p class=\"summary\">Adds a payOut update function that credits the calling principal with 10,000 tokens. Uses Motoko's shared(msg) syntax to read msg.caller, tests it from the CLI and the frontend (two different callers), then adds a disabled button, a once-only claim check, and result text shown on the button.</p>\n<h3>payOut skeleton</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a public function payOut taking no arguments and returning async Text. First version just returns &quot;Success&quot;; the frontend will display whatever string comes back on the button.</li><li>Unlike balanceOf this is not a query: it changes state, so it is an update call and takes a couple of seconds to complete.</li></ul>\n<h3>shared(msg) and msg.caller</h3>\n<ul><li>Every public actor method is already callable by other actors/users (shared by default), but writing the shared keyword explicitly lets you name an implicit message parameter, conventionally msg.</li><li>msg.caller holds the Principal of whoever invoked the method: a user identity or another canister.</li><li>Declare it as public shared (msg) func payOut() : async Text.</li><li>Debug.print(debug_show(msg.caller)) prints the caller; it needs import Debug from mo:base/Debug. debug_show converts any value to text. The output appears in the terminal running dfx start, not in the command that made the call.</li><li>Test A, CLI: dfx canister call token payOut returns Success and the replica log shows your own identity principal.</li><li>Test B, frontend: import the generated bridge in Faucet.jsx and call await token.payOut() in the click handler. The log now shows a different, anonymous principal because the browser user has no logged-in identity. This shows msg.caller changes with the caller.</li></ul>\n<h3>Crediting the caller</h3>\n<ul><li>balances.put(msg.caller, 10000) adds the caller to the ledger with the faucet amount (the page promises 10,000 tokens).</li><li>Verify with the Balance panel: a fresh anonymous principal shows 0, then after clicking the faucet button it shows 10,000. The owner still shows the original billion.</li></ul>\n<h3>Disabling the button while the call runs</h3>\n<ul><li>Because the update takes seconds, repeated clicks should be blocked. Add const [isDisabled, setDisable] = useState(false) (import useState) and set disabled={isDisabled} on the button.</li><li>Call setDisable(true) at the start of handleClick. Optionally call setDisable(false) afterwards; leaving it disabled is reasonable since each user should claim once.</li><li>A page refresh resets React state, so the button becomes clickable again; the real protection must live in the backend.</li></ul>\n<h3>Preventing repeat claims in the backend</h3>\n<ul><li>Without a check, someone could spend their tokens and claim again, giving away free supply and damaging the token's economics.</li><li>balances.get(msg.caller) returns null when the principal is not in the ledger. If it is null, add the 10,000 and return &quot;Success&quot;; otherwise return an 'already claimed' message.</li><li>Both branches return Text, so the frontend can show either one.</li></ul>\n<h3>Showing the result on the button</h3>\n<ul><li>Add const [buttonText, setText] = useState(&quot;Gimme gimme&quot;) and render {buttonText} as the button label.</li><li>In handleClick: const result = await token.payOut(); setText(result).</li><li>Redeploy with dfx deploy after changing main.mo. Because the HashMap is not persisted yet, every deploy resets balances (a later lesson fixes persistence), so a fresh test starts at 0 for the anonymous user.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">payOut in main.mo (reference repo version, comment/unused code trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;\n// ... existing owner / balances / balanceOf / getSymbol ...\n\n  public shared (msg) func payOut() : async Text {\n    Debug.print(debug_show (msg.caller));   // prints in the `dfx start` terminal\n    if (balances.get(msg.caller) == null) {\n      let amount = 10000;\n      balances.put(msg.caller, amount);\n      return &quot;Success&quot;;\n    } else {\n      return &quot;Already Claimed&quot;;\n    };\n  };</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Faucet.jsx after the lesson</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { token } from &quot;../../../declarations/token&quot;; // reference repo: DANG_backend\n\nfunction Faucet() {\n  const [isDisabled, setDisable] = useState(false);\n  const [buttonText, setText] = useState(&quot;Gimme gimme&quot;);\n\n  async function handleClick() {\n    setDisable(true);\n    const result = await token.payOut();\n    setText(result);\n    // setDisable(false); // optional: re-enable after completion\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;blue window&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;label&gt;Get your free tokens here! Claim 10,000 DANG tokens to your account.&lt;/label&gt;\n      &lt;p className=&quot;trade-buttons&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;button id=&quot;btn-payout&quot; onClick={handleClick} disabled={isDisabled}&gt;\n          {buttonText}\n        &lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Faucet;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Call from the CLI</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx deploy\ndfx canister call token payOut</pre>\n<h2>The task: Build the faucet</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> main.mo with balances, balanceOf and getSymbol; Faucet.jsx with a button that does nothing useful.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Each distinct principal can claim 10,000 tokens exactly once from the UI, with the button reflecting the outcome.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add public shared (msg) func payOut() : async Text returning &quot;Success&quot;.</li><li>Import Debug and print msg.caller; deploy and call payOut from the CLI to see your principal in the dfx start log.</li><li>Import the token bridge in Faucet.jsx and await token.payOut() inside handleClick; click the button and note the anonymous principal in the log.</li><li>Replace the print-only body with balances.put(msg.caller, 10000).</li><li>Check the anonymous principal in the Balance panel before and after the click.</li><li>Add isDisabled state, bind disabled={isDisabled} and set it true on click.</li><li>Wrap the put in if (balances.get(msg.caller) == null) and return Success or an already-claimed message.</li><li>Add buttonText state and set it from the returned result; dfx deploy and test twice (refresh the page to re-enable the button).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> First claim: button reads Success and the balance becomes 10,000. After a refresh, a second click shows the already-claimed text and the balance is unchanged.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;\n// ... existing owner / balances / balanceOf / getSymbol ...\n\n  public shared (msg) func payOut() : async Text {\n    Debug.print(debug_show (msg.caller));   // prints in the `dfx start` terminal\n    if (balances.get(msg.caller) == null) {\n      let amount = 10000;\n      balances.put(msg.caller, amount);\n      return &quot;Success&quot;;\n    } else {\n      return &quot;Already Claimed&quot;;\n    };\n  };</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting dfx deploy after changing main.mo; npm start does not do this.</li><li>Looking for Debug.print output in the browser or CLI-call terminal instead of the dfx start terminal.</li><li>Assuming the disabled button is security: reloading resets it, so enforce once-only claims in Motoko.</li><li>Mismatch between method name in Motoko and CLI/JS (payOut spelling).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo imports DANG_backend from declarations; use your canister name. Its shared (msg) line is written 'public shared (msg)'. It also keeps the frontend variable named isDisable, which differs from the lecture's isDisabled.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l353", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds the Motoko transfer function that moves tokens between two principals, after a short demo showing that msg.caller changes to the canister itself when one canister function calls another. Then wires a Transfer React component with controlled inputs, a disabled button while waiting, and a hidden feedback paragraph.</p>\n<h3>Who is msg.caller when a function calls another function?</h3>\n<ul><li>Demo: temporarily strip transfer down to a Debug.print of the caller plus a call to payOut(), then run dfx deploy and trigger each path to compare the printed principal.</li><li>Calling payOut from the command line (dfx canister call &lt;canister&gt; payOut) prints the principal of the identity controlling the terminal.</li><li>Calling payOut from the frontend button prints the principal of the website user.</li><li>Calling transfer (which internally calls payOut) prints neither of those: it prints the canister's own ID, the same ID shown in the 'Upgrading code for canister ...' line during dfx deploy.</li><li>Takeaway: inside a canister, an internal call to another public function is made by the canister (the whole actor), so msg.caller there is the canister, not the original user.</li><li>Consequence for design: do not call a shared function like payOut from transfer and expect the user identity to carry through. Use the non-shared helper (balanceOf is a plain query that takes the principal as an argument) and pass msg.caller explicitly.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Check the spelling and capitalization of method names on the CLI; the lecture notes the capital O in payOut.</li></ul>\n<h3>Signature of transfer</h3>\n<ul><li>Declared as public shared (msg) func transfer(to : Principal, amount : Nat) : async Text. shared (msg) exposes the caller; the return is a Text status message like payOut.</li><li>There is no 'from' parameter: the sender is always msg.caller, so a user can only spend from their own account. This is what makes the function safe to expose.</li><li>to is a Principal (the long textual ID the user pastes in) and amount is a Nat.</li><li>Until the body is complete, add a placeholder return &quot;Success&quot; at the bottom so the type checker stops complaining while you write the logic.</li></ul>\n<h3>Transfer logic step by step</h3>\n<ul><li>Read the sender balance: let fromBalance = await balanceOf(msg.caller). balanceOf is an async query function, so the call must be awaited, otherwise the compiler reports a type error (an async value is not a Nat).</li><li>Guard: only proceed if fromBalance &gt; amount; otherwise return the text 'Insufficient Funds'. (Extra) The reference repo uses strict greater-than, so sending exactly your whole balance is rejected; &gt;= would allow it.</li><li>Compute let newFromBalance : Nat = fromBalance - amount. The explicit Nat annotation is needed because Motoko's subtraction could in principle go below zero and warns otherwise; the guard already guarantees it cannot.</li><li>Write it back with balances.put(msg.caller, newFromBalance), which replaces the existing entry for that key in the HashMap.</li><li>Read the recipient balance with await balanceOf(to). balanceOf already returns 0 for principals not in the map, so brand-new recipients work without extra code.</li><li>Compute newToBalance = toBalance + amount and store it with balances.put(to, newToBalance).</li><li>Return 'Success'. The whole function is just a subtraction, an addition and two ledger updates.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Because there are awaits between the reads and the writes, concurrent calls could interleave; for a production token the balance changes should be done without an await in between.</li></ul>\n<h3>Transfer component state and wiring</h3>\n<ul><li>Two controlled inputs: recipientId/setId and amount/setAmount, both useState(&quot;&quot;). Each input has value={...} and onChange={(e) =&gt; setX(e.target.value)} so state always holds the current text.</li><li>The same pattern was used for the balance checker; it keeps the latest values available when the button is clicked.</li><li>Import the backend actor from the generated declarations folder (path three levels up, in /declarations, named for the canister) and call await backend.transfer(to, amount). Method names are case sensitive.</li><li>Type conversion is required: input values are strings. Import Principal from @dfinity/principal and use Principal.fromText(recipientId) for the first argument, and Number(amount) for the second.</li><li>Spelling mistakes in state variable names (the instructor hit one with amount) must be fixed in every place it is used.</li></ul>\n<h3>Testing the flow</h3>\n<ul><li>Run dfx deploy first; redeploying resets the in-memory ledger, so balances start at 0.</li><li>Claim from the faucet (10,000 tokens), copy a target principal, transfer 50, then re-check both balances: the sender drops by 50 and the recipient shows 50.</li><li>Check the recipient by pasting their principal into the balance checker.</li></ul>\n<h3>UX touches</h3>\n<ul><li>Disable the button while the call is in flight: isDisable state (initially false) bound to the button's disabled attribute; set true at click start and false after the result arrives.</li><li>Capture the returned text: const result = await transfer(...), store it in a feedback state (initially empty string) and render it in a paragraph.</li><li>Hide the paragraph initially with an isHidden state (initially true) bound to the hidden attribute; set it to true at the start of each click and to false once feedback is set, so stale messages vanish.</li><li>Test both outcomes: 'Success', and transferring more than you hold (e.g. 100,000 with a 10,000 balance) gives 'Insufficient Funds' after a pause.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Demo to reveal msg.caller for internal calls</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public shared (msg) func transfer() : async Text {\n  Debug.print(debug_show (msg.caller));  // caller of transfer\n  let r = await payOut();                // payOut prints ITS msg.caller\n  return &quot;Success&quot;;\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final transfer function (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public shared (msg) func transfer(to : Principal, amount : Nat) : async Text {\n  let fromBalance = await balanceOf(msg.caller);\n  if (fromBalance &gt; amount) {\n    let newFromBalance : Nat = fromBalance - amount;\n    balances.put(msg.caller, newFromBalance);\n\n    let toBalance = await balanceOf(to);\n    let newToBalance : Nat = toBalance + amount;\n    balances.put(to, newToBalance);\n\n    return &quot;Success&quot;;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;Insufficient Funds&quot;;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Transfer component logic (JSX trimmed to key parts)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Transfer() {\n  const [recipientId, setId] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [amount, setAmount] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [isDisable, setDisable] = useState(false);\n  const [feedback, setFeedback] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [isHidden, setHidden] = useState(true);\n\n  async function handleClick() {\n    setHidden(true);\n    setDisable(true);\n    const recipient = Principal.fromText(recipientId);\n    const amountToTransfer = Number(amount);\n    const result = await DANG_backend.transfer(recipient, amountToTransfer);\n    setFeedback(result);\n    setHidden(false);\n    setDisable(false);\n  }\n  // inputs: value={recipientId} onChange={(e) =&gt; setId(e.target.value)}\n  //         value={amount}      onChange={(e) =&gt; setAmount(e.target.value)}\n  // button: onClick={handleClick} disabled={isDisable}\n  // feedback: &lt;p hidden={isHidden}&gt;{feedback}&lt;/p&gt;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Implement token transfer (backend and UI)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project from the previous lesson with balanceOf, getSymbol, payOut and a Transfer component that has inputs but no logic.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Let the signed-in user send an amount of tokens to any principal and see the outcome in the page.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Demo (optional): in transfer, Debug.print msg.caller and call payOut; deploy and compare the principal from CLI, frontend and internal call.</li><li>Make transfer public shared (msg) with params to : Principal and amount : Nat returning async Text.</li><li>Fetch fromBalance with await balanceOf(msg.caller).</li><li>If fromBalance &gt; amount, subtract with an explicit Nat type and balances.put for msg.caller.</li><li>Fetch toBalance with await balanceOf(to), add amount, balances.put for to; return &quot;Success&quot;.</li><li>Else return &quot;Insufficient Funds&quot;.</li><li>In Transfer.jsx add useState for recipientId and amount and bind to the inputs.</li><li>In handleClick convert with Principal.fromText and Number, then await the backend transfer.</li><li>Add isDisable, feedback and isHidden state to disable the button, show the result and hide it between clicks.</li><li>Run dfx deploy, claim from faucet, transfer 50, verify both balances; then try an oversized amount.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Sender balance drops, recipient balance rises, button greys out during the call, and the page shows Success or Insufficient Funds.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public shared (msg) func transfer(to : Principal, amount : Nat) : async Text {\n  let fromBalance = await balanceOf(msg.caller);\n  if (fromBalance &gt; amount) {\n    let newFromBalance : Nat = fromBalance - amount;\n    balances.put(msg.caller, newFromBalance);\n\n    let toBalance = await balanceOf(to);\n    let newToBalance : Nat = toBalance + amount;\n    balances.put(to, newToBalance);\n\n    return &quot;Success&quot;;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;Insufficient Funds&quot;;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting await on balanceOf, which gives a type error.</li><li>Passing the raw string or the input text instead of Principal.fromText / Number to the backend.</li><li>Assuming msg.caller is the user inside a function reached via an internal call.</li><li>Redeploying resets the HashMap, so balances go back to zero.</li><li>Misspelled state variable or method names (the generated method is case sensitive).</li><li>Using &gt; rather than &gt;= means an exact full-balance transfer fails.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l354", "<p class=\"summary\">Replaces the faucet's direct ledger write with a call to transfer so tokens come out of the canister's own balance instead of being created. Because the canister starts with zero, the owner funds it once from the command line.</p>\n<h3>Why the faucet had to change</h3>\n<ul><li>The old payOut did balances.put(msg.caller, 10000), which mints 10,000 new tokens per claim and silently inflates the supply set at 1 billion.</li><li>Better model: the canister holds a pool of tokens and each claim moves 10,000 from the canister to the claimant, so the total stays constant.</li><li>This needs transfer to exist and work, which it now does.</li></ul>\n<h3>Updating payOut</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside the 'not yet claimed' branch, replace balances.put with let result = await transfer(msg.caller, amount). The recipient is the caller of payOut and the amount is the existing 10,000 constant.</li><li>transfer is async, so it must be awaited; its Text result is returned directly in place of the hard-coded 'Success'. payOut therefore reports 'Success' or 'Insufficient Funds' (if the pool runs dry).</li><li>The 'Already Claimed' branch is unchanged.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why it initially fails</h3>\n<ul><li>After dfx deploy, clicking the faucet button returns 'Insufficient Funds'.</li><li>Reason: as shown in the previous lecture, a function called from another function sees the canister itself as msg.caller. So transfer debits the token canister's account, which holds 0.</li><li>Confirm by pasting the token canister's ID into the balance checker: it shows 0 DANG. The deploy output lists the canister ID under 'Upgrading code for canister token' (a separate 'token assets' canister is also listed; use token).</li><li>Do not hard-code the canister ID into main.mo: it is unknown before the first deploy and may change between deployments.</li></ul>\n<h3>Funding the canister from the command line</h3>\n<ul><li>Commands (also in the project README under 'Charge the Canister'): get the ID with dfx canister id token.</li><li>Store it as a Candid principal literal in a shell variable: CANISTER_PUBLIC_KEY=&quot;principal \\&quot;$( dfx canister id token )\\&quot;&quot;. The command substitution inserts the ID inside principal &quot;...&quot; syntax automatically.</li><li>Check with echo $CANISTER_PUBLIC_KEY; it should print principal &quot;&lt;id&gt;&quot; in lowercase.</li><li>Call dfx canister call token transfer &quot;($CANISTER_PUBLIC_KEY, 500_000_000)&quot;. Candid args are passed as a tuple in parentheses, and underscores are allowed in numeric literals for readability.</li><li>The terminal identity is the owner, who holds the whole initial supply, so as msg.caller they are the sender. The reply should be 'Success'.</li><li>Refresh the site and check the canister ID in the balance checker: it now shows 500,000,000 DANG.</li><li>Claiming from the faucet now works and returns 'Success'.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Every dfx deploy resets the HashMap in this version, so the funding step has to be repeated after each deploy until persistence is added (next lecture).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo also changed the guard in transfer from &gt; to &gt;= in this lesson, so a sender can move their exact full balance.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">payOut using transfer</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public shared (msg) func payOut() : async Text {\n  if (balances.get(msg.caller) == null) {\n    let amount = 10000;\n    let result = await transfer(msg.caller, amount);\n    return result;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;Already Claimed&quot;;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Fund the canister from the terminal</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx canister id token\nCANISTER_PUBLIC_KEY=&quot;principal \\&quot;$( \\dfx canister id token )\\&quot;&quot;\necho $CANISTER_PUBLIC_KEY\ndfx canister call token transfer &quot;($CANISTER_PUBLIC_KEY, 500_000_000)&quot;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Make the faucet draw from the canister's balance</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Working project from the previous lesson with transfer and a payOut that writes directly to the ledger.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Faucet claims move tokens from the canister to the user without increasing total supply.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In payOut replace balances.put with let result = await transfer(msg.caller, amount).</li><li>Return result instead of the literal 'Success'.</li><li>Run dfx deploy and try the faucet; observe 'Insufficient Funds'.</li><li>Look up the canister ID and confirm via the balance checker that it has 0.</li><li>Run the four terminal commands to put 500,000,000 tokens into the canister.</li><li>Refresh and claim again to get 'Success' and check both balances.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Canister balance falls by 10,000 and the claimant gains 10,000 on each new claim.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public shared (msg) func payOut() : async Text {\n  if (balances.get(msg.caller) == null) {\n    let amount = 10000;\n    let result = await transfer(msg.caller, amount);\n    return result;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;Already Claimed&quot;;\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to fund the canister after each redeploy.</li><li>Using the wrong canister name (token vs token assets, or the project's actual backend name).</li><li>Typing the principal without the principal &quot;...&quot; wrapper in the CLI.</li><li>Hard-coding the canister ID in source.</li><li>Method name case, e.g. payOut.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l355", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows that redeploying with code changes wipes the in-memory HashMap ledger, then fixes it by copying the map into a stable array in preupgrade and rebuilding it in postupgrade. Also moves the initial owner balance into guarded initialisation and tightens visibility.</p>\n<h3>The problem: state resets on upgrade</h3>\n<ul><li>Demo: fund the canister with 500,000,000 tokens, claim from the faucet (balance drops), then add a harmless Debug.print to main.mo and run dfx deploy again. The canister's balance is back to 0.</li><li>The code change must be real; a comment does not trigger an upgrade, but a print statement does.</li><li>Canister lifecycle: created if missing, code installed, and upgraded when the source changes. On upgrade, every non-stable variable is re-initialised from its initial expression, so balances goes back to a fresh map holding only the owner's supply.</li><li>Side note: owner and totalSupply never change, so they can be let constants rather than var.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why a HashMap cannot simply be stable</h3>\n<ul><li>Adding the stable keyword to balances produces a compile error: HashMap is not a stable type (it contains mutable internal state and functions).</li><li>Stable types include arrays of stable elements, tuples, Nat, Text, Principal, etc.</li><li>Idea: use a stable array of (Principal, Nat) tuples as a temporary carrier. Declare private stable var balanceEntries : [(Principal, Nat)] = [] with the tuple element types mirroring the map's key and value.</li><li>Why not use the array permanently: lookups and updates (balances.get/put) would require a linear scan of the array, which is slow and, on the Internet Computer, costs cycles. The map gives fast key lookups, so convert only around upgrades.</li></ul>\n<h3>System functions preupgrade and postupgrade</h3>\n<ul><li>system func preupgrade() and system func postupgrade() are special hooks the runtime calls just before the old code is replaced and just after the new code starts. They must be spelled exactly and need the system keyword in front of func; copy-paste to avoid typos. Each declaration needs a trailing semicolon inside the actor.</li><li>preupgrade: balanceEntries := Iter.toArray(balances.entries()). Use := to reassign. HashMap.entries() returns an iterator of (key, value) pairs; Iter.toArray (import mo:base/Iter) collects an iterator into an array. The map itself is not iterable, hence entries().</li><li>After preupgrade finishes, the upgrade resets balances to an empty map, but stable balanceEntries survives.</li><li>postupgrade: balances := HashMap.fromIter&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(balanceEntries.vals(), 1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash). fromIter takes an iterator (arrays expose one via .vals()), the initial capacity, the key-equality function and the hash function, the same last three arguments used when creating the map. Type parameters give the key and value types.</li><li>balances therefore must be declared with var (reassignable), not as an immutable value.</li></ul>\n<h3>Initial owner balance</h3>\n<ul><li>The line balances.put(owner, totalSupply) at actor top level runs on every upgrade, which would keep resetting the ledger to the owner holding everything.</li><li>Fix: wrap it in if (balances.size() &lt; 1) { balances.put(owner, totalSupply) } and place it inside postupgrade, so it only seeds the ledger when the restored map is empty.</li><li>Also keep the same guarded block at top level after balances is declared, because a fresh project that is deployed for the first time never goes through postupgrade; this way the owner still starts with the supply.</li><li>Result: only transfer (used by payOut and users) changes balances afterwards.</li></ul>\n<h3>Encapsulation</h3>\n<ul><li>Mark balances and balanceEntries as private. Only code inside the actor can touch them; balances is changed only by transfer, and balanceEntries only by the upgrade hooks.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Actor members that are not public are private by default; the explicit keyword documents intent.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verification</h3>\n<ul><li>Repeat the experiment: fund canister, claim from faucet (down 10,000), add a Debug.print, deploy again, refresh the site, check the same canister ID. The balance is preserved.</li><li>If you lose the canister ID, rerun dfx canister id token.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo is written for newer Motoko where the actor is declared persistent actor and the rebuilt map is marked transient var; lecture code uses a plain actor with stable var. In the persistent form, any non-transient var is persisted automatically, but the HashMap still has to be transient and rebuilt, so the same pre/postupgrade approach applies.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Upgrade hooks can trap if the data is large; for big ledgers newer approaches (stable data structures) avoid copying everything.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Pattern as built in the lecture (plain actor)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Iter &quot;mo:base/Iter&quot;;\nimport HashMap &quot;mo:base/HashMap&quot;;\n\nactor Token {\n  let owner : Principal = Principal.fromText(&quot;&lt;owner principal&gt;&quot;);\n  let totalSupply : Nat = 1000000000;\n\n  private stable var balanceEntries : [(Principal, Nat)] = [];\n  private var balances = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n\n  if (balances.size() &lt; 1) {\n    balances.put(owner, totalSupply);   // first-ever deploy, no upgrade happens\n  };\n\n  // ... balanceOf, getSymbol, payOut, transfer ...\n\n  system func preupgrade() {\n    balanceEntries := Iter.toArray(balances.entries());\n  };\n\n  system func postupgrade() {\n    balances := HashMap.fromIter&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(balanceEntries.vals(), 1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n    if (balances.size() &lt; 1) {\n      balances.put(owner, totalSupply);\n    };\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Newer syntax used in the reference repo</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">persistent actor Token {\n  let owner : Principal = Principal.fromText(&quot;...&quot;);\n  let totalSupply : Nat = 1000000000;\n  let symbol : Text = &quot;DANG&quot;;\n  private stable var balanceEntries : [(Principal, Nat)] = [];\n  private transient var balances = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n  // pre/postupgrade identical to above\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Make the token ledger survive upgrades</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project from the previous lesson, where a redeploy zeros the balances.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Balances and the funded canister stay intact after dfx deploy with code changes.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Reproduce the bug: fund the canister, claim, add a Debug.print, redeploy, see 0.</li><li>Import Iter from mo:base/Iter.</li><li>Add private stable var balanceEntries : [(Principal, Nat)] = [].</li><li>Make balances a private var HashMap.</li><li>Write preupgrade to store Iter.toArray(balances.entries()) in balanceEntries.</li><li>Write postupgrade to rebuild balances with HashMap.fromIter and balanceEntries.vals().</li><li>Move the owner seeding into an if (balances.size() &lt; 1) block inside postupgrade and also keep one after the map declaration.</li><li>Change owner/totalSupply/symbol to let.</li><li>Redeploy twice and confirm balances are retained.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Balances persist across upgrades; fresh installs still give the owner the full supply.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import Iter &quot;mo:base/Iter&quot;;\nimport HashMap &quot;mo:base/HashMap&quot;;\n\nactor Token {\n  let owner : Principal = Principal.fromText(&quot;&lt;owner principal&gt;&quot;);\n  let totalSupply : Nat = 1000000000;\n\n  private stable var balanceEntries : [(Principal, Nat)] = [];\n  private var balances = HashMap.HashMap&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n\n  if (balances.size() &lt; 1) {\n    balances.put(owner, totalSupply);   // first-ever deploy, no upgrade happens\n  };\n\n  // ... balanceOf, getSymbol, payOut, transfer ...\n\n  system func preupgrade() {\n    balanceEntries := Iter.toArray(balances.entries());\n  };\n\n  system func postupgrade() {\n    balances := HashMap.fromIter&lt;Principal, Nat&gt;(balanceEntries.vals(), 1, Principal.equal, Principal.hash);\n    if (balances.size() &lt; 1) {\n      balances.put(owner, totalSupply);\n    };\n  };\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Testing with a comment-only change, which does not trigger an upgrade.</li><li>Misspelling preupgrade/postupgrade or omitting system.</li><li>Declaring balances with let so it cannot be reassigned in postupgrade.</li><li>Trying to mark the HashMap stable.</li><li>Leaving the unconditional balances.put(owner, totalSupply) so upgrades reset the owner.</li><li>Forgetting that dfx deploy --mode reinstall (or a fresh start) still wipes stable data.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l356", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why the token app needs real user authentication and introduces Internet Identity, the Internet Computer's anonymous, passwordless login system based on WebAuthn and device-held keys.</p>\n<h3>The problem in the current app</h3>\n<ul><li>Without login, every visitor reaches the backend as the anonymous principal, so everyone shares one identity: claims and transfers are not tied to a real user.</li><li>The same login flow is familiar from signing in to the NNS dapp or claiming free cycles: it prompts for an Internet Identity and a device check such as a security key or laptop biometrics.</li></ul>\n<h3>What Internet Identity is</h3>\n<ul><li>An authentication framework for the Internet Computer that plays the role 'Sign in with Google/Facebook' plays elsewhere, but without a third party learning who you are.</li><li>Rationale: storing credentials safely is hard and breaches are common, so delegating it is attractive. OAuth with big providers does that but exposes your activity to them. Internet Identity delegates it while staying anonymous.</li><li>You can create any number of identity 'anchors'. Each acts as a pseudonym for logging into dapps. No username, password, email, seed phrase, one-time code or authenticator app is requested.</li><li>Result: services cannot link a person across apps; each app sees a different application-specific principal derived from the identity.</li></ul>\n<h3>How it works</h3>\n<ul><li>Built on the Web Authentication API (WebAuthn), a W3C and FIDO specification supported by major browsers and operating systems.</li><li>Secured with the Internet Computer's chain-key cryptography; devices with fingerprint, face unlock or hardware keys act as authenticators.</li><li>Adding a device to an anchor generates a key pair. The public key is stored on the blockchain; the private key stays inside the device, protected by its biometric or PIN.</li><li>Logging in to a dapp needs both parts to authorise a session, and the result is a pseudonymous, per-application identity (principal) delivered to the app.</li><li>Users avoid remembering credentials, removing the weakest link in typical account security.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Dapps do not receive the anchor number, only the derived principal; the frontend gets it via the @dfinity/auth-client library, covered next.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Treating the anonymous principal as a real user (all visitors share it).</li><li>Confusing the anchor number with the principal a dapp sees.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l357", "<p class=\"summary\">Adds an Internet Identity login step to the React entry file using AuthClient from the auth-client package, skips the login when a cached session exists, and renders the app only after authentication. The backend still sees an anonymous caller until the next lesson.</p>\n<h3>Starting point</h3>\n<ul><li>The frontend entry file (main.jsx in the reference repo, called index.jsx in the lecture) currently just renders the App component into the root element. App composes Header, Faucet, Balance and Transfer.</li><li>The @dfinity/auth-client package (with its supporting identity packages from DFINITY) provides the login class and opens the standard Internet Identity UI, so no login screen has to be written.</li></ul>\n<h3>AuthClient.create and login</h3>\n<ul><li>Import { AuthClient } from &quot;@dfinity/auth-client&quot;.</li><li>Inside an async init function, const authClient = await AuthClient.create(). create is asynchronous because it loads any stored session/key from the browser.</li><li>Call await authClient.login({ identityProvider, onSuccess }). identityProvider is the URL of the Internet Identity service that supplies the login page (reference repo: https://identity.ic0.app/#authorize). onSuccess is a callback run after login succeeds.</li><li>Move the existing render call into onSuccess so the app only appears for authenticated users.</li><li>login opens a popup, so the browser must allow popups for localhost (a 'popup blocked' message appears otherwise); refresh after allowing.</li><li>In the popup choose Authenticate, confirm with the device (e.g. Touch ID). A recovery-method prompt can be skipped with 'add recovery later'. The final screen shows which site is requesting the login and which identity is used; proceed to return to the app.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Local dfx development typically still uses the mainnet identity service URL this way; a locally deployed identity canister would need a different URL.</li></ul>\n<h3>Skipping login when already authenticated</h3>\n<ul><li>Without a check, every page load triggers login again. Use await authClient.isAuthenticated() (method on the AuthClient) to see if a valid session exists.</li><li>The session is cached in the browser, similar to staying logged in to webmail; coming back within about eight days should not ask again (as per the lecture).</li><li>Logic: if authenticated, go straight to the app; else call login, whose onSuccess goes to the same place.</li><li>Factor the rendering into async function handleAuthenticated(authClient) that creates the React root and renders App (inside StrictMode in the reference repo). Call it from both branches. The authClient parameter is not used yet but will be needed to obtain the identity next lesson.</li><li>Call init() at the end of the file.</li></ul>\n<h3>Current limitation</h3>\n<ul><li>Even when logged in, clicking the faucet still says 'Already Claimed': the actor calls made by the generated declarations still use an anonymous identity, so msg.caller in payOut/transfer is the anonymous principal.</li><li>Passing the authenticated identity to the backend actor is the topic of the next lesson ('Live Deployment to Test Internet Identity Authentication').</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Entry file with Internet Identity login (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom/client&quot;;\nimport App from &quot;./App&quot;;\nimport { AuthClient } from &quot;@dfinity/auth-client&quot;;\n\nconst init = async () =&gt; {\n  const authClient = await AuthClient.create();\n\n  if (await authClient.isAuthenticated()) {\n    handleAuthenticated(authClient);\n  } else {\n    await authClient.login({\n      identityProvider: &quot;https://identity.ic0.app/#authorize&quot;,\n      onSuccess: () =&gt; {\n        handleAuthenticated(authClient);\n      },\n    });\n  }\n};\n\nasync function handleAuthenticated(authClient) {\n  ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)).render(\n    &lt;React.StrictMode&gt;\n      &lt;App /&gt;\n    &lt;/React.StrictMode&gt;\n  );\n}\n\ninit();</pre>\n<h2>The task: Gate the app behind Internet Identity login</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> React frontend that renders App immediately on load.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show the app only after the user logs in with Internet Identity, and skip the popup for returning users.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Import AuthClient from @dfinity/auth-client.</li><li>Create async init: const authClient = await AuthClient.create().</li><li>Check await authClient.isAuthenticated().</li><li>If true call handleAuthenticated(authClient).</li><li>Otherwise await authClient.login with identityProvider URL and an onSuccess that calls handleAuthenticated(authClient).</li><li>Define async handleAuthenticated that renders App into #root.</li><li>Call init(); allow popups from localhost and test login, then refresh to confirm it is skipped.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> First visit opens the Internet Identity popup; after authenticating the app loads; reloads go straight to the app.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom/client&quot;;\nimport App from &quot;./App&quot;;\nimport { AuthClient } from &quot;@dfinity/auth-client&quot;;\n\nconst init = async () =&gt; {\n  const authClient = await AuthClient.create();\n\n  if (await authClient.isAuthenticated()) {\n    handleAuthenticated(authClient);\n  } else {\n    await authClient.login({\n      identityProvider: &quot;https://identity.ic0.app/#authorize&quot;,\n      onSuccess: () =&gt; {\n        handleAuthenticated(authClient);\n      },\n    });\n  }\n};\n\nasync function handleAuthenticated(authClient) {\n  ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)).render(\n    &lt;React.StrictMode&gt;\n      &lt;App /&gt;\n    &lt;/React.StrictMode&gt;\n  );\n}\n\ninit();</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Popup blockers stop the login window.</li><li>Forgetting await on AuthClient.create or isAuthenticated (a promise is always truthy).</li><li>Leaving the render call outside the callback so the app shows before login.</li><li>Expecting the backend to see the logged-in principal; identity is not passed to the actor yet.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l358", "<p class=\"summary\">Wires the Internet Identity login into the token app: Faucet and Transfer build an authenticated actor so msg.caller becomes the logged-in user's principal. Because the local dfx replica cannot complete the Internet Identity sign-off, the project is deployed to the live Internet Computer to test it, and the user's principal is surfaced in the UI via props.</p>\n<h3>Goal: make msg.caller the authenticated principal</h3>\n<ul><li>Until now Faucet.jsx called payOut() on the imported default actor (token), which talks to the canister anonymously, so the caller was always the anonymous principal.</li><li>To pay out to the real logged-in user, the component needs its own actor that carries the user's identity, then calls payOut() on that actor instead.</li><li>Inside the Motoko payOut, msg.caller then resolves to the authenticated user's principal, and that account receives the tokens.</li></ul>\n<h3>Imports needed in the component</h3>\n<ul><li>AuthClient comes from the @dfinity/auth-client package (already used in index.jsx for login).</li><li>From the generated declarations folder, import canisterId and createActor in addition to the default token actor. These already exist in the declarations index.js generated for the project.</li><li>createActor builds a canister handle (an 'actor') for a given canister id; it accepts an options object. canisterId is filled in from an environment variable at build time, holding the backend canister's principal.</li></ul>\n<h3>Building the authenticated actor inside the click handler</h3>\n<ul><li>After the button is disabled, call await AuthClient.create() to get a client. It restores the session saved by the earlier login.</li><li>Call await authClient.getIdentity() to get the identity object of the logged-in user. Both awaits are required; forgetting one yields a promise instead of the value.</li><li>Call createActor(canisterId, { agentOptions: { identity } }). The second argument is the options object; agentOptions is passed to the underlying HTTP agent, which signs requests with that identity.</li><li>Store the result in a const such as authenticatedCanister and call authenticatedCanister.payOut() in place of token.payOut().</li><li>Typo-prone spots: createActor, getIdentity, matching braces and commas around the nested objects.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why it cannot be tested locally</h3>\n<ul><li>Clicking the faucet button against the local dfx replica returns a 403 error. The local replica is a simulation for development; the Internet Identity sign-off flow only works against the live network.</li><li>So the project must be deployed to the mainnet ('ic' network). Enough cycles are needed; if low, convert ICP to cycles as shown in earlier lessons.</li><li>Follow the README step by step by copying commands rather than retyping them, since they are error-prone.</li></ul>\n<h3>Live deployment steps</h3>\n<ul><li>Deploy all canisters: dfx deploy --network ic. Takes a couple of minutes if cycles suffice.</li><li>Find the live backend canister id (differs from the local one): dfx canister --network ic id token.</li><li>Wrap it in a Candid principal literal and store in a shell variable (LIVE_CANISTER_KEY), then echo it to check that the format is principal &quot;...&quot;.</li><li>Transfer half the supply to the canister so the faucet has tokens to give out: dfx canister --network ic call token transfer with the variable and the amount. A success result confirms it. The owner keeps the other half.</li><li>Find the frontend (assets) canister id: dfx canister --network ic id token_assets. Open it in a browser as &lt;id&gt;.raw.ic0.app, which serves the deployed React app.</li><li>Allow pop-ups first: the Internet Identity login opens in a pop-up window. On the first visit the user must authenticate again, because the live site is a different origin from the local one.</li><li>Click the faucet button: success may take longer than locally because the live network latency varies. A success message proves the authenticated path works, in contrast to the earlier error.</li></ul>\n<h3>Inspecting the identity and getting the principal</h3>\n<ul><li>Debug.print output from Motoko does not appear in a terminal on the live network, so logging is done on the frontend instead.</li><li>console.log(await authClient.getIdentity()) in the login handler shows an identity object with four main fields; the one of interest is _principal.</li><li>Take const identity = await authClient.getIdentity(); then const userPrincipal = identity._principal.toString(). The raw property is an object; toString() converts it to the familiar readable text id.</li><li>Checking that principal's balance in the Balance component shows the 10,000 tokens claimed from the faucet, confirming the call was made as the authenticated user.</li><li>After each frontend change, redeploy with dfx deploy --network ic and refresh.</li></ul>\n<h3>Showing the principal in the UI with props</h3>\n<ul><li>Pass the string down as a prop: in index.jsx render &lt;App loggedInPrincipal={userPrincipal} /&gt;. The pattern is propName={value}.</li><li>In App, read props.loggedInPrincipal and hand it to the faucet as &lt;Faucet userPrincipal={props.loggedInPrincipal} /&gt;.</li><li>Faucet takes props as its function parameter (a common typo is writing 'pops') and renders {props.userPrincipal} in the label text instead of the generic 'your account' wording.</li><li>Result: the faucet label displays the logged-in user's principal id without opening the console.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Faucet.jsx: claim through an authenticated actor</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { canisterId, createActor } from &quot;../../../declarations/token&quot;;\nimport { AuthClient } from &quot;@dfinity/auth-client&quot;;\n\nfunction Faucet(props) {\n  const [isDisable, setDisable] = useState(false);\n  const [buttonText, setText] = useState(&quot;Gimme gimme&quot;);\n\n  async function handleClick() {\n    setDisable(true);\n    const authClient = await AuthClient.create();\n    const identity = await authClient.getIdentity();\n    const authenticatedCanister = createActor(canisterId, {\n      agentOptions: { identity },\n    });\n    const result = await authenticatedCanister.payOut();\n    setText(result);\n  }\n  // ...label shows {props.userPrincipal}\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.jsx: derive the principal string and pass it down</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">async function handleAuthenticated(authClient) {\n  const identity = await authClient.getIdentity();\n  const userPrincipal = identity._principal.toString();\n  ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)).render(\n    &lt;React.StrictMode&gt;\n      &lt;App loggedInPrincipal={userPrincipal} /&gt;\n    &lt;/React.StrictMode&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Live network commands (from the project README)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx deploy --network ic\ndfx canister --network ic id token\nLIVE_CANISTER_KEY=&quot;principal \\&quot;$( dfx canister --network ic id token )\\&quot;&quot;\necho $LIVE_CANISTER_KEY\ndfx canister --network ic call token transfer &quot;($LIVE_CANISTER_KEY, 500_000_000)&quot;\ndfx canister --network ic id token_assets\n# open https://&lt;assets-id&gt;.raw.ic0.app</pre>\n<h2>The task: Hook the Transfer component up to the authenticated canister</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Faucet already uses the authenticated actor and shows the principal. Transfer.jsx still calls token.transfer on the anonymous default actor, so transferring from the logged-in account fails with insufficient funds.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Transfer tokens from the logged-in user's account to another principal (for example an earlier anonymous account) using the same authentication pattern as the Faucet.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In Transfer.jsx, import AuthClient from @dfinity/auth-client and canisterId plus createActor from the declarations; the old token import can be dropped.</li><li>In the click handler, just before the transfer call, create the client with await AuthClient.create().</li><li>Get the identity with await authClient.getIdentity().</li><li>Build const authenticatedCanister = createActor(canisterId, { agentOptions: { identity } }).</li><li>Replace token.transfer(...) with authenticatedCanister.transfer(recipient, amountToTransfer), keeping the recipient principal and amount from the form.</li><li>Deploy to the live network, refresh, enter a recipient principal and an amount, press Transfer.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> After a noticeable wait the feedback says Success instead of Insufficient funds; the sender's balance drops by the amount sent (200 in the demo) and the recipient's balance rises accordingly.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">async function handleClick() {\n  setHidden(true);\n  setDisable(true);\n  const recipient = Principal.fromText(recipientId);\n  const amountToTransfer = Number(amount);\n\n  const authClient = await AuthClient.create();\n  const identity = await authClient.getIdentity();\n  const authenticatedCanister = createActor(canisterId, {\n    agentOptions: { identity },\n  });\n\n  const result = await authenticatedCanister.transfer(recipient, amountToTransfer);\n  setFeedback(result);\n  setHidden(false);\n  setDisable(false);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Testing locally: the Internet Identity sign-off gives a 403 on the local replica; deploy to the ic network.</li><li>Missing an await on AuthClient.create() or getIdentity().</li><li>Forgetting to redeploy after each frontend change, or running low on cycles.</li><li>Popups blocked, so the login window never appears.</li><li>Writing props as 'pops' or mismatching the prop name between index.jsx, App and Faucet.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo is a newer dfx scaffold: canisters are DANG_backend and DANG_frontend (not token and token_assets), it has a typo prop loogedInPrincipal used consistently, and Faucet is the only component that displays the principal.</li></ul>");
